# Dependencies
#
# `/vendor/` is anchored, and that leading slash is load-bearing. Unanchored,
# it matches at ANY depth, and Composer's directory name is not rare: it
# silently swallowed `public/vendor/pdfjs`, the PDF.js viewer every fee-voucher
# screen loads. Git reported a clean working tree while the files existed on one
# laptop and nowhere else — no error, no warning, and a blank frame on CI and in
# production. Anchor these, and the rule says what it means: Composer's vendor
# directory, at the root, not any directory anywhere that shares its name.
node_modules/
/vendor/
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Build output — anchored for the same reason. `build/`, `dist/` and `out/` are
# even likelier to collide, since almost every vendored JS library ships one.
/dist/
/build/
/out/
*.tsbuildinfo
coverage/
.next/
.nuxt/

# Environment & secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# The production TEMPLATE, which carries no values — only the keys, the required
# settings and the reasoning. `.env.*` above would otherwise swallow it, and a
# template nobody can find is a template nobody uses.
!.env.production.example
*.pem
*.key
secrets.*

# Logs
*.log
# Anchored, and for exactly the reason the /vendor/ note above gives. Unanchored
# `logs/` matches at ANY depth, so it swallowed `storage/logs/` — the directory
# Laravel writes laravel.log into. Every other storage directory ships a
# `.gitignore` placeholder so a fresh clone has it; that one could not, because
# this rule ignored the placeholder too.
#
# The consequence was not cosmetic. `git clone` into /var/www/institute (which
# is step 10 of the production bootstrap) produced a tree with no storage/logs,
# so ReadinessController found a required path missing and /ready answered 503 —
# meaning the health check at the end of the very first deploy on a fresh box
# would have failed, on a brand-new correctly-provisioned machine. CI caught it
# because CI is the only place that ever runs from a genuinely clean checkout.
/logs/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# OS & editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# VSCode (keep shared settings, ignore local)
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/launch.json

# Package manager
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/releases

# Laravel
/public/build
/public/hot
/public/storage
/storage/*.key
/storage/pail
# The default BACKUP_LOCK_PATH (config/backup.php). Production points it at
# /run/institute-backup.lock instead; this is the laptop/test-suite fallback.
/storage/framework/institute-backup.lock
/bootstrap/cache/*.php
_ide_helper.php
.phpunit.result.cache
.phpunit.cache/
/auth.json
Homestead.json
Homestead.yaml
/.nova
/.zed
/.phpactor.json
database/database.sqlite

# Local worktrees & tooling scratch
.claude/
.playwright-mcp/

# Debugging screenshots. Two of these (challan-view.png, students-azeem.png)
# were committed to the repository root from a browser-testing session and sat
# there for a fortnight. They are working notes, not source: unreferenced by any
# view, controller or document, and each one a 136KB binary that every clone
# pays for forever. Screenshots belong in the pull request, not in the tree.
/*.png
/*.jpg
/*.jpeg
/*.webp
/screenshot*
# ...but the application's own assets are source and must stay. The rules above
# are anchored to the repository root precisely so they cannot reach these.
!/public/assets/*.png

# Design handoff bundle (reference only — large binaries, not app source)
/bbt-pos-student-registration-frontend/

# Institute data exports and issued invoices. These carry real student names,
# phone numbers and fee history, so they stay out of the repository even
# though the importer reads them from here.
/student_details_report*.xlsx
/Invoice *.pdf

# The institute's hand-kept intake sheets, sent one month at a time while the
# POS was offline. Same reason as above, and worse: these carry CNIC numbers,
# which the export tool never included.
/*Enrollment*.xlsx
/*Enrolment*.xlsx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documentation and environment templates are kept LOCAL, not published.
#
# Untracked on 2026-08-29 at the owner's request. The files still exist in every
# working copy that had them and in git history before that commit — this only
# stops them being pushed.
#
# Two consequences worth knowing, because neither announces itself:
#
#   1. A FRESH CLONE HAS NO DOCUMENTATION AND NO .env TEMPLATE. The deployment
#      path (docs/DEPLOYMENT.md §13, checklist P3-07) copies
#      .env.production.example on the server. A clean clone cannot, so that file
#      has to reach the box another way — scp it, or paste it.
#
#   2. `deploy.sh` and `provision.sh` still refer people to docs/DEPLOYMENT.md
#      and docs/PRODUCTION-EMERGENCY.md in their error messages. Those pointers
#      are now to files a fresh clone does not have.
#
# CI does not depend on any of this: the `Prepare environment` step in
# .github/workflows/ci.yml writes its own four-line .env rather than copying
# .env.example, precisely so removing these could not break the test matrix.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.md
/docs/
.env.example
.env.production.example
